public class Ops {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(7 / 2);          // 3   (integer division)
        System.out.println(7 / 2.0);        // 3.5
        System.out.println(7 % 3);          // 1
        System.out.println((int) 3.99);     // 3   (cast truncates)
        System.out.println(Math.round(3.5)); // 4

        int score = 10;
        score += 5;                          // 15
        String s = null;
        boolean empty = s != null && s.isEmpty(); // short-circuit: no NPE
        String label = score >= 15 ? "pass" : "fail";
        System.out.println(score + " " + empty + " " + label); // 15 false pass
    }
}

public class Strings {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String name = "  Ada Lovelace ";
        String clean = name.strip();                  // "Ada Lovelace"
        name.toUpperCase();                           // result ignored: name unchanged

        String typed = new String("Ada Lovelace");    // a different object
        System.out.println(clean == typed);           // false
        System.out.println(clean.equals(typed));      // true

        System.out.println(clean.substring(0, 3));    // Ada
        System.out.println(String.join("|", clean.split(" "))); // Ada|Lovelace

        var sb = new StringBuilder();
        for (int i = 1; i <= 3; i++) sb.append(i).append(',');
        System.out.println(sb);                       // 1,2,3,

        String html = """
            <p>Hello, %s</p>
            """.formatted(clean);
        System.out.print(html);                       // <p>Hello, Ada Lovelace</p>
    }
}

concepts.body:
An `if` statement runs a block when a boolean condition is true, with optional `else if` and `else` branches. Always use braces, even for one line — it prevents bugs when someone adds a second statement later.

When you branch on one value against several constants, use `switch`. Modern Java (14+) has the **arrow form**, which does not fall through and can be used as an **expression** that yields a value:

```java
String type = switch (day) {
    case SATURDAY, SUNDAY -> "weekend";
    default -> "weekday";
};
```

- Several labels can share one case, separated by commas.
- A switch expression must be **exhaustive**: every possible input needs a case, usually via `default`. For enums the compiler accepts listing every constant instead.
- If a case needs several statements, use a block and `yield` the result: `case 3 -> { log(); yield "three"; }`.

You can switch on `int`, `char`, `String`, enums and (since Java 21) any type with pattern-matching cases, which you'll meet later.

The old colon form `case 1:` still works but falls through into the next case unless you write `break`. Forgetting `break` is a classic bug, so prefer arrows in new code.

For simple two-way choices that produce a value, the ternary `cond ? a : b` is often clearest. For complex conditions, extract them into a well-named boolean variable or method so the `if` reads like a sentence.

