    }
}

concepts.body:
A **constructor** runs when an object is created with `new`. It has the class's name and no return type:

```java
class Account {
    private final String owner;
    private long balanceCents;

    Account(String owner, long openingCents) {
        this.owner = owner;
        this.balanceCents = openingCents;
    }
}
```

If you write no constructor, the compiler adds a no-argument **default constructor**. As soon as you write any constructor, that default disappears — a common surprise when a framework later needs a no-arg one.

You can **overload** constructors and have one delegate to another with `this(...)`, which must be the first statement. Keep the real logic in the most complete constructor and let the others supply defaults:

```java
Account(String owner) { this(owner, 0); }
```

Validate arguments in the constructor and fail fast, so an invalid object can never exist. `Objects.requireNonNull(owner, "owner")` and an `IllegalArgumentException` for bad values are the standard tools.

`final` fields must be assigned exactly once, in a constructor or initializer. Making fields final wherever possible gives you objects that are easier to reason about and safe to share between threads.

When a class has many optional parameters, long constructor overload chains become unreadable. Options are a static factory method with a descriptive name (`Account.empty(owner)`), a builder, or — for plain data — a record, which generates the constructor for you.

Avoid calling overridable methods from a constructor: a subclass override would run before the subclass's own fields are initialised.

