import java.util.HashSet;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Set;

public class Records {
    record Point(int x, int y) {
        Point {                                   // compact constructor
            if (x < 0 || y < 0) throw new IllegalArgumentException("negative");
        }
        double distanceTo(Point o) { return Math.hypot(x - o.x, y - o.y); }
    }
    record Order(String id, List<String> items) {
        Order { items = List.copyOf(items); }     // defensive copy
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        var a = new Point(3, 4);
        System.out.println(a);                    // Point[x=3, y=4]
        System.out.println(a.x() + " " + a.distanceTo(new Point(0, 0))); // 3 5.0

        Set<Point> seen = new HashSet<>();
        seen.add(new Point(1, 2));
        System.out.println(seen.contains(new Point(1, 2))); // true: value equality
    }
}

import java.io.BufferedReader;
import java.io.IOException;
import java.io.StringReader;

public class Errors {
    static class ConfigException extends RuntimeException {
        ConfigException(String msg, Throwable cause) { super(msg, cause); }
    }
    static int readPort(String text) {
        try (var reader = new BufferedReader(new StringReader(text))) {
            return Integer.parseInt(reader.readLine().strip());
        } catch (IOException | NumberFormatException e) {
            throw new ConfigException("invalid port: " + text, e); // keep the cause
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(readPort("8080"));          // 8080
        try {
            readPort("eighty");
        } catch (ConfigException e) {   // invalid port: eighty <- NumberFormatException
            System.out.println(e.getMessage() + " <- " + e.getCause().getClass().getSimpleName());
        }
    }
}

concepts.body:
**Generics** let a class or method work with a type that is chosen by the user. `List<String>` is a list that holds only strings: the compiler rejects `add(42)` and `get` returns a `String` with no cast. Before generics (Java 5), collections held `Object` and every read needed a cast that could fail at runtime.

The **diamond** `<>` lets the compiler infer type arguments: `Map<String, List<Integer>> m = new HashMap<>();`.

Type arguments must be reference types: write `List<Integer>`, not `List<int>`; autoboxing handles the conversion.

Writing your own generic class is simple — declare a type parameter after the name:

```java
class Box<T> {
    private T value;
    void set(T v) { value = v; }
    T get() { return value; }
}
```

By convention type parameters are single capital letters: `T` (type), `E` (element), `K`/`V` (key/value), `R` (result).

A **generic method** declares its own type parameter before the return type: `static <T> T firstOrNull(List<T> list)`. The compiler infers `T` from the arguments at each call site.

You can restrict a type parameter with a **bound**: `<T extends Comparable<T>>` means "any T that can compare itself to another T", which lets the method call `compareTo`.

Avoid **raw types** like plain `List` without a type argument. They exist only for compatibility with ancient code, disable the checks and produce "unchecked" warnings. Wildcards (`? extends`, `? super`) and type erasure are covered in the advanced generics lesson.

